Let the no-cURL build compile

q3rally's own additions to ioquake3 - the ladder logger, the version check and
the download manager - have never been built with `USE_CURL` off. Each reaches a
cURL-only declaration from code that is not cURL-only:

  sv_ladder.c    SV_LadderLogSubmitSnapshot defined inside #ifdef USE_CURL
                 (opened at :1558), called at :2531 at conditional depth 0
  cl_update.c    CL_UpdateVersionCheck_Fail defined inside #ifdef USE_CURL
                 (opened at :61), called at :244 inside #ifndef USE_CURL - the
                 branch whose whole job is to report "cURL support not compiled"
  cl_downloads.c curl_off_t used at :147 and :394 at conditional depth 0, so the
                 file needs cURL's header to declare its own structures

None is a portability problem; all three are ordinary build breaks on a
configuration upstream ships a switch for.

Each is fixed the way its own intent points rather than uniformly:

**`sv_ladder.c` guards the call.** Without cURL there is no transport to submit a
snapshot over, so there is nothing to do.

**`cl_update.c` moves the helper out of the block.** The !USE_CURL path needs it
to say why the check is unavailable, and it depends only on setters already
outside the guard. Its one cURL-specific line, the handle cleanup, stays guarded
in place.

**`cl_downloads.c` defines the type when cURL is absent.** Nothing crosses an ABI
boundary on that path - the progress callback is only ever handed to cURL - so a
local definition of the same width is enough, and it keeps the cURL-enabled build
byte-identical.

diff --git a/engine/code/client/cl_downloads.c b/engine/code/client/cl_downloads.c
index 1ef8dc6..4fce4e7 100644
--- a/engine/code/client/cl_downloads.c
+++ b/engine/code/client/cl_downloads.c
@@ -57,6 +57,12 @@ Foundation, Inc., 51 Franklin St, Fifth Floor, Boston, MA  02110-1301  USA
 #ifdef USE_CURL_DLOPEN
 #include "../sys/sys_loadlib.h"
 #endif
+#else
+/* This module's own structures and the progress callback are declared with curl_off_t at file
+   scope, outside any USE_CURL guard, so the file does not compile without cURL's header. Nothing
+   crosses an ABI boundary on this path - the callback is only ever handed to cURL - so a local
+   definition of the same width is enough to keep the no-cURL build honest. */
+typedef long long curl_off_t;
 #endif
 
 // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
